Description:
The increasing use of machine learning in systems development, particularly in safety-critical domains such as avionics and ground transportation means that guarantees about the characteristics of learned components must be a vital part of the development process. For example, we may want safety guarantees about the physical effects of a neural network steering a car, such as absence of collisions, and stability guarantees to tolerate unexpected disturbance. Formal modeling and verification are promising tools to establish such guarantees. The predictive nature of formal models makes them attractive as sources for reward functions in formal-assisted machine learning, but todays verification tools and machine learning tools are largely disconnected. Existing work on [Provably Safe Neural Network Controllers](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10998) attempts to analyze learned systems with an independently developed formal model. In this project, we propose to reverse this process and use formal models as a starting point for generating reward functions:

Develop a syntactic conventions to identify reward functions in formal models
Safety rewards
Progress rewards
Stability rewards
Reward shaping
Implement a tool to generate reward functions from analytic models
Concrete implementation efforts related to these tasks will use Python and [Gymnasium](https://gymnasium.farama.org/) for their popularity in reinforcement learning. The project will also base on the Gymnasium Synthesis library . The anticipated outcome of this project is a code generator that translates rewards in formal models into reward functions in Python/Gymnasium.

We propose to extend an existing formal programming language (differential dynamic logic) with syntactic conventions in comments to enable generating reward functions. For example, in previous work we identified elements of a formal model with their connection to reinforcement learning as follows:

 

```java

// @Learn

def controlFunc ::= {

u := *;

}

 

// @Environment

def stepFunc ::= { x’=v, v’=a }

```

 

We propose

 

```java

// @Ensure

def terminate(Real x, Real o) <-> x != o;

 

// @Reach

def progressReward(Real x, Real v, Real n, Real g) := x+n*v + v^2/2*b >= g

 

// @Avoid

def safetyReward(Real v) := v;

 

// @Shape
 
def safetyShape(Real x) := if x>=0 then 1/x else x;

```

We then use the syntactic conventions in the programming language to generate Python code; for example, `@Ensure` defines an early termination condition for learning episodes; `@Reach` tells us what goal to achieve, `@Avoid` defines safety conditions that must be satisfied by the learned agent, `@Shape` allows us to adjust the functional shape of a progress or safety reward (e.g., to prefer being close to the safety boundary when safe, such as staying close to the speed limit). We expect to be able to synthesize reach-avoid rewards from stability and attractivity proofs, which requires extending the formal runtime monitoring technique ModelPlex to the shapes of stability and attractivity proofs.

The anticipated outcome of this project are syntactic conventions for formal models, and a software tool to turn such formal models into Python code for the Gymnasium reinforcement learning library.

