Title Idea:
Bridging Formal Verification and Reinforcement Learning: Synthesizing Provably Informed Reward Functions from Differential Dynamic Logic

Abstract
The integration of Neural Network (NN) controllers into safety-critical Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) requires rigorous guarantees that traditional "black-box" machine learning methods cannot provide. While recent advancements like VerSAILLE and Mosaic have enabled the verification of NN controllers against formal specifications in Differential Dynamic Logic ($dL$), a significant disconnect remains: formal models and reinforcement learning (RL) environments are typically developed in isolation. This research proposes a novel "formal-first" approach to RL by reversing the verification pipeline. Instead of verifying a pre-trained agent, we propose to synthesize the RL training environment—specifically the reward functions and termination conditions—directly from $dL$ formal models.

We introduce a set of syntactic conventions for $dL$ that allow developers to annotate formal specifications with RL-specific metadata, including safety constraints (@Avoid), liveness goals (@Reach), and episode termination logic (@Ensure). Building upon the Gymnasium Synthesis library, we develop a transpiler that translates these annotated formal models into executable Python code for the Gymnasium RL framework. A key contribution of this work is the extension of the ModelPlex runtime monitoring technique to synthesize "reward shaping" functions (@Shape) from stability and attractivity proofs. This ensures that the learned agent is not only incentivized to reach its goal but is also guided by the underlying mathematical structure of the safety proof. The anticipated outcome is a software toolchain that automates the creation of provably informed RL environments, reducing the gap between formal methods and autonomous systems development.

Key Elements Included:
The Problem: The gap between formal verification (which happens after training) and RL training (which often lacks formal rigor).
The Solution: A "transpiler" that turns $dL$ models into Gymnasium environments.
The Innovation: Using annotations like @Reach, @Avoid, and @Shape to automate reward engineering.
Technical Basis: Mentions ModelPlex, Differential Dynamic Logic, and Gymnasium, which were the core components of your provided text.
Outcome: A toolchain that makes RL environments "provably informed" by the safety proofs.
